follow existing style. I'll add:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def positive_int_or_none(*, minimum: int = 1) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            try:\n                value = int(v)\n            except ValueError:\n                raise ValueError(f\"must be an integer, got {v!r}\") from None\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"must be >= {minimum}, got {value}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nThen in envs class add:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.positive_int_or_none(minimum=32)\n```\nNeed to check envs class structure — the class name is `envs`, with properties declared like `FOO = EnvVar.int(default=1)` presumably with comments. Let me check the file to see envs body. I read environ.py fully before (registered full read) but current context doesn't contain contents. I need to read the relevant ranges to place the edit — that's fine, reading the envs class region.\n\nNote: empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError -> good, message includes... wrapped with name by __get__. Good.\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with max_message_chars keyword; validate int >= 32, not bool; truncate.\n\nImplementation:\n\n```python\n    _TRUNCATE_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n    def __init__(self, fmt, datefmt=None, high_precision=False, use_color=False, *, max_message_chars=None):\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        self.high_precision = high_precision\n        self.use_color = use_color\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nformat: need to compute message after args substitution without mutating record. Use `record.getMessage()`. Then if budget set and len(message) > budget: truncate. Then produce truncated msg on a copy of the record so super().format uses it? Simpler: compute truncated message, then temporarily work with a shallow copy of record:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        message = record.getMessage()\n        truncated = self._truncate(message)\n        if truncated is not message:\n            record = copy.copy(record)  # but copy module not in allowed imports!\n```\nAllowed imports: collections, datetime, itertools, json, math, queue, random, re, stat, statistics, time, unicodedata. Hmm — the allowed imports list applies to my action code, not to the file edits. But safer: avoid `copy` module; I can't add imports? The task says \"可增加必要标准库导入\". But I'm limited in action code imports, not file content. Still, to be safe use `logging.makeLogRecord(record.__dict__)`? That copies dict; but args remain. Alternative: create a copy via `logging.LogRecord` subclass trick. Simplest: \n\n```python\n        if truncated != message:\n            record = logging.makeLogRecord(dict(record.__dict__))\n            record.msg = truncated\n            record.args = ()\n```\nmakeLogRecord with dict that has 'msg','args' etc. — makeLogRecord creates LogRecord(None,None,\"\",0,\"\",(),None,None) then `rv.__dict__.update(dict)`. That works and includes message? record.__dict__ contains 'message' only after getMessage? Actually getMessage doesn't set record.message; Formatter.format sets record.message. So dict copy is fine. Then set msg=truncated, args=(). Careful: dict(record.__dict__) copies exc_info, stack_info fine.\n\nBut careful: `record.message` might be set by other handlers that formatted earlier — the copy would carry message, and Formatter.format recomputes: it does `record.message = record.getMessage()` so fine.\n\nkeep_full_message: skip truncation if `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True`.\n\nTruncation math: budget B, marker length M=15. keep = B - M. head = ceil(keep/2) = (keep+1)//2, tail = keep - head. return message[:head] + marker + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\").\n\nFor B=32: keep=17, head=9, tail=8. ✓\n\nDefault logger init: read config:\n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\nNeed import: in logger.py how are env configs accessed? Probably `from deepinfer.environ import envs`? Need to check imports of logger.py. Let me read lines 1-35 of logger.py.\n\n3. stats_logger.py: `logger.info(m, extra={\"keep_full_message\": True})`.\n\nWait — but the interactive integration test extracts the queue consumer function verbatim and runs it with a fake logger. So `extra={\"keep_full_message\": True}` must be in `_log_worker`.\n\nLet me now read the needed pieces: logger.py lines 1-32, environ.py lines 200-260ish and the envs class body. Actually I need to see envs class to add a field. Let me read environ.py lines 90-260.\n\nAlso stats_logger imports lines 1-35 already exposed partially; need env import? Let's read logger.py 1-32 and environ.py 94-260."}, "logprobs": null, "finish_reason": "stop"}], "usage": {"prompt_tokens": 7470, "completion_tokens": 1656, "total_tokens": 9126, "prompt_tokens_details": {"cached_tokens": 4096}, "completion_tokens_details": {"reasoning_tokens": 1579}, "prompt_cache_hit_tokens": 4096, "prompt_cache_miss_tokens": 3374}, "system_fingerprint": "bc19ba97cbe016479903222bb1578db6"}, "classification": "ok"}
